// @log 123
// @log 705032827
// @hint redundant type annotation: `fi32` is inferred as `i32`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `fi64` is inferred as `i64`
// `x as% T` is the WRAP cast, the fourth member of the `as` family — DECISIONS.md
// §"`as%` is the wrap cast, the fourth member of the `as` family" (owner, 2026-09-04). It
// never fails, so it has no null, no trap and no propagation: it keeps the target width's low
// bits, two's complement.
//
// The rows of the ruling's table, once as literals (which the checker folds, so a consumer can
// write a 32-bit bit pattern) and once through passthrough calls, where nothing folds:
//
//   i64 as% i32   the low 32 bits          i32/i64 as% u8   the low 8 bits
//   i32 as% i64   sign-extend              i32/i64 as% same identity
//
// The literal half is what glean's VL-023 and VL-044 asked for: `0xb81a1aaa` is typed `i64`
// because its value does not fit an `i32`, and every other cast is exact-or-fail, so the one
// spelling that looks like "reinterpret these 32 bits" was the one that could not.
function fi32(x: i32): i32 { x }
function fi64(n: i64): i64 { n }

// Folded literals — the constant is the value, not a runtime conversion.
print(0xb81a1aaa as% i32)
print(300 as% u8)
print((0 - 1) as% u8)
print((1 << 40) as% i32)
print(5000000000 as% i32)
print(7 as% i64)
print(9 as% i32)

// The same rows through calls, so the conversion opcode is what answers.
print(fi64(5000000000) as% i32)
print(fi64(0 - 1) as% i32)
print(fi32(300) as% u8)
print(fi64(1000) as% u8)
print(fi32(7) as% i64)
print(fi64(9) as% i64)
print(fi32(11) as% i32)

// An un-annotated parameter is pinned by its call site, and the two spellings must agree.
function lowByte(a) { a as% u8 }
function low32(a) { a as% i32 }
print(lowByte(fi64(5000000123)))
print(low32(fi64(5000000123)))
